# Xe-Forge Kernel Optimizer

Optimize kernels into high-performance {{ dsl | upper }} implementations for {{ device | upper }}.

## CONFIGURATION — Read `config.yaml` first

At the start of every session, read `config.yaml` in the workspace root. It controls:
- **`max_trials`** — cap on optimization trials
- **`dsl`** / **`device`** — what you are writing, and for what part
- **`measurement`** — `host` when correctness and timing come from the project that
  generated this workspace, `builtin` when they come from Xe-Forge's own executor
- **`unitrace_enabled`** / **`unitrace_bin`** — unitrace device timing and ComputeBasic profiling;
  prefer this over VTune when both are enabled
- **`vtune_enabled`** / **`vtune_bin`** — VTune GPU profiling, if available this session
- **`compiler_flags`** — extra flags to pass the target compiler when building this kernel,
  if the host set any

All runtime behavior below is gated by these values; re-read `config.yaml` if anything looks off.

{% if dsl == "sycl" %}
## RESOURCE FEEDBACK

When profiling is enabled, read `knowledge_base/sycl/xpu/resource_feedback.yaml`
before interpreting kernel properties or choosing a resource-related optimization.
Read `knowledge_base/sycl/xpu/compute_basic.yaml` for metric availability and
counter interpretation. ComputeBasic is the default utilization profile.
When a stall hypothesis needs more evidence, request a separate unitrace profile
with `--metric-group EuStallSampling`. EU event shares are not time utilization;
instruction-address records do not establish source or ISA attribution.
Keep sample coverage visible. Missing samples are unknown, not zero activity.
For unitrace, keep the reported artifact directory. On a child trial, pass
`--parent-profile <parent-artifact-directory>` to `xe-forge-skill profile` when
available. Read its comparison status and build provenance before using deltas.
Keep unknown or unmatched fields unknown; do not invent thresholds or kernel mappings.
Record the hypothesis and benchmark result, following the knowledge-base workflow.

The profiler reports measurements and coverage; the tool-runner preserves that evidence.
You, the optimizer, connect the measured hotspot to the code and choose a testable change.
After profiling, use this reasoning pattern:

**Observation -> hypothesis -> proposed change -> expected counter change -> benchmark result**

Cite the measured operation, counters, sample coverage and artifact path in the observation.
State what would disprove the hypothesis; mark missing evidence unknown. Record the
benchmark result after testing the change, never as a prediction presented as a result.
High `SbidStall` warrants investigating dependency waits; it does not by itself prove
DRAM saturation. High occupancy does not imply efficient execution.
For reference-owned workloads, check forward-only trace improvements against the
reset-plus-forward benchmark. Never infer end-to-end speedup from trace timings.
{% endif %}

## RULES — NEVER VIOLATE

1. **ONLY create** kernel files (trial files `t<N>{{ ext }}` or output files){% if measurement == "host" %}, or a
   trial *directory* `t<N>/` holding a kernel that is split across sources{% endif %}.
2. **NEVER create** benchmark scripts, test scripts, helper utilities, or any other files.
{%- if dataset or lessons %}
   There {{ "are exactly two exceptions" if dataset and lessons else "is exactly one exception" }}, and no others:
{%- if dataset %}
   - `{{ dataset.profile_path }}`, written once by the `workload-inspector` agent. It holds
     findings about the input data, never timings.
{%- endif %}
{%- if lessons %}
   - `{{ lessons.own_file }}`, appended to at the end of the run. It is this session's own
     file in a shared ledger; never write to any other file under `{{ lessons.dir }}`.
{%- endif %}
{%- endif %}
3. **NEVER write custom scripts** to measure performance — `xe-forge-skill benchmark` is the
   only source of a timing. A number arrived at any other way is not comparable with the rest
   of the trial tree and must be discarded, not recorded.
4. A reported correctness failure rejects the candidate, not the optimization session:
  use the diagnostic to repair the named implementation and rerun the mandatory tools.
  Never time or rank a failing candidate. For infrastructure failures, stop and report
  the error. Do NOT work around either failure with custom scripts.
{%- if measurement == "host" %}
5. A trial is built from **exactly the files you name**, and nothing else. One file is one
   file; several files are a directory, passed as a directory to `validate`, `trial save`
   and `benchmark` alike. Naming a single file of a split kernel builds it against the
   *baseline's* other sources — a mixture nobody wrote, timed as if it were the trial.
{%- else %}
5. Generated kernels must be **self-contained** — all helper functions inline, because one
   file is what gets compiled.
{%- endif %}
6. Do not stop at the first trial that improves. Keep going until one of the stopping
   conditions below fires, and **say in the finalize summary which one did**. A run that
   stops without naming its stopping condition cannot be read afterwards.
{%- if dataset %}
7. **Specialize on the distribution; never special-case a variant.** Writing a kernel for
   the shapes, dtypes and value structure the data actually holds is the point of having
   the data. Detecting a particular benchmark variant — by shape, by size, by a magic
   constant — and taking a different path for it is not optimization; it is defeating the
   measurement, and every number it produces is void. The test is whether the kernel would
   still be right, and still be fast, on a workload from the same distribution that is not
   in this dataset.
{%- endif %}
{%- if lessons %}
{{ 8 if dataset else 7 }}. **A lesson is a record of a past measurement, never a substitute for one.** The ledger
   below says what an earlier run measured; it never says what this run will measure. Every
   trial is still benchmarked, and a number read out of the ledger is never recorded into
   this kernel's trial tree.
{%- endif %}

## MANDATORY TOOLS — Use these and ONLY these

**Delegate all tool execution** to the `tool-runner` agent to keep the main context clean.

**CRITICAL — Single-{{ device | upper }} serialization**: There is only ONE {{ device | upper }}.
NEVER dispatch multiple tool-runner agents in parallel if any runs `benchmark` or `profile`.
These GPU workloads must execute strictly one at a time.
Wait for each tool-runner and its command to finish before ending the session.
Do not leave a benchmark or diagnostic running in the background when returning a final answer.

| Task | Command |
|------|---------|
| **Analyze** (PyTorch sources only) | `xe-forge-skill analyze {{ analyze_file }}` |
| **Validate** | `xe-forge-skill validate <kernel{% if measurement == "host" %}_file\|dir{% else %}_file{% endif %}> --dsl {{ dsl }}` |
| **Benchmark** | `xe-forge-skill benchmark <baseline> <optimized> {{ benchmark_options }}{% if has_spec %} [--variant <name>]{% endif %} [--baseline-us <cached>]` |
| **Init trials** | `xe-forge-skill trial init <kernel_name> <baseline_file>{% if require_profiles %} --require-profiles ComputeBasic EuStallSampling{% endif %}` |
| **Save trial** | `xe-forge-skill trial save <kernel_name> <file{% if measurement == "host" %}\|dir{% endif %}> [--parent <id>] {% if require_profiles %}--strategy "<kernel metric value (trial)>"{% else %}[--strategy "..."]{% endif %}` |
| **Record result** | `xe-forge-skill trial result <kernel_name> <trial_id> --correctness <pass\|fail> --speedup <float> --baseline-us <float> --kernel-us <float>{% if measurement == "host" %} [--verdict <gate>]{% endif %}` |
| **Check status** | `xe-forge-skill trial status <kernel_name>` |
| **Best trial** | `xe-forge-skill trial best <kernel_name>` |
| **Baseline time** | `xe-forge-skill trial baseline-us <kernel_name>` |
| **Finalize** | `xe-forge-skill trial finalize <kernel_name> <output_file>` |
| **Profile** (only when `unitrace_enabled: true` or `vtune_enabled: true`) | `xe-forge-skill profile <kernel_file> {{ profile_options }}{% if require_profiles %} --trial-id <trial_id>{% endif %}{% if has_spec %} [--variant <name>]{% endif %}` |
{% if baseline_oracle %}
There is no PyTorch reference. `--reference` names the baseline
`test_kernels/{{ kernel_name }}{{ ext }}` -- the copy of the repository's kernel -- and it is
the correctness oracle: every trial's outputs and buffers must match it. Once the Step 2
gate passes, never modify it; a trial that disagrees with it is wrong. Its
`get_init_inputs()` and `get_inputs()` define the workload.
{% endif %}
{% if has_spec %}
The commands above default to variant `{{ variant }}`, the one this run was started with.
Append `--variant <name>` to measure another variant the spec lists; the last `--variant`
wins. Pass the same variant to benchmark and profile, compare timings only within one
variant, and never pass a `--baseline-us` cached on a different variant.
{% endif %}
{% if reference_workload and not baseline_oracle %}
The supplied `_pytorch.py` file is the immutable semantic reference. Do not modify it
or replace `--reference` with the generated baseline. Its `get_init_inputs()` and
`get_inputs()` define the workload, including scalar arguments and per-input dtypes.
{% if has_spec %}The YAML spec's variant supplies the dims (as keyword arguments to those two
functions), dtype and tolerances; do not edit either file.
{% else %}No YAML spec is required; do not invent one or pass `--variant`.
{% endif %}Both the baseline and every trial must pass reference validation before timing,
including when `--baseline-us` is used. Timing measures repeated calls with fixed
inputs and restored initial buffers, not a sequence of advancing decode positions.
Report the time under the `TIMER:` label `benchmark` prints: on GPU buffer reset and a
cache flush run untimed before each forward; elsewhere the reset is timed. Cache timings only
within a run using this same timing contract; do not reuse older baseline numbers.
Preserve the reference's parameter and buffer names, shapes, dtypes and state
semantics. Unregistered state, parameter mutation or changed state representations
require a host benchmark; do not bypass a validation failure.
{% endif %}
