## THE EXISTING KERNEL — it lives in another project, and you may read it

`{{ kernel_name }}` is not written here. It already exists in `{{ kernel_repo }}`, with
its own build target, and — maybe — its own test and benchmark. Optimizing it means
improving what is really there, not a guess at what a kernel by this name usually looks
like.

- **Read-only.** Never modify `{{ kernel_repo }}`. Copy what you need out of it; do not
  edit it in place.
- **The profile is the map, not the territory.** `{{ kernel_locator_profile_path }}`
  names the real implementation file, its build target and its existing test/benchmark
  (or says plainly that one is missing). Trust it over guessing from the op's name.
- **If no benchmark exists**, write one yourself, following the closest sibling pattern
  the profile points at, before starting the trial loop — an unmeasured op cannot be
  optimized, only guessed at.
{% endif %}
{% if dataset %}
## THE WORKLOAD DATA — it is on disk, and you may read it

The benchmark variants in the spec are not shapes someone chose. They come from a dataset
the project that generated this workspace has on disk, and that dataset holds the
workloads themselves: which combinations occurred, how often, and the tensors behind
them. You can open it.

- **Dataset**: `{{ dataset.path }}`
{%- if dataset.definition %}
- **Definition**: `{{ dataset.definition }}`
{%- endif %}
{%- if dataset.input_fidelity == "captured" %}
- **Input fidelity**: `captured` — the tensors are the ones the model ran on. What you read
  about their values is evidence about the real workload.
{%- elif dataset.input_fidelity == "shapes-only" %}
- **Input fidelity**: `shapes-only` — the shapes are the ones the model ran; the values are
  generated. Anything you conclude about value range, sparsity or numerical conditioning is
  a property of the generator, not of the workload, and is not a reason to specialize.
{%- endif %}
{%- if dataset.variants %}
- **Variant → workload** (the variant name is what `benchmark --variant` takes):

| Variant | Workload |
|---------|----------|
{%- for name, workload in dataset.variants.items() %}
| `{{ name }}` | `{{ workload }}` |
{%- endfor %}
{%- endif %}

Three things bound what this is for:

- **It is read-only.** Never write to it. It is the ground truth correctness is judged
  against, shared with everything else the host runs.
- **An inspection is not a measurement.** No amount of reading the data produces a
  timing, a speedup or a correctness verdict. `xe-forge-skill benchmark` produces those
  and nothing else does — Rule 3 is unchanged by the dataset being readable.
- **Specialize, do not special-case.** Rule 7.

Delegate the reading to the `workload-inspector` agent, once, in Step 1. It writes
`{{ dataset.profile_path }}`; every later step reads that file rather than the dataset.
{% endif %}
{%- if lessons %}
## WHAT EARLIER RUNS MEASURED — the lessons ledger

`{{ lessons.dir }}` holds what previous optimization runs measured **on this part**. It
outlives this workspace, which is scratch, and it is the only place a finding from a run
that has already been paid for can reach the next one.

It is not the knowledge base. Those entries are patterns recorded on the vendor's
machines, which is why you do not read their `expected_speedup`. Every line in the ledger
came from `xe-forge-skill benchmark` here.

- **Read every `*.md` in it**, in Step 1 — including the files for other kernels. How this
  part rewards or punishes a strategy usually outlives the kernel it was learned on.
- **Write only `{{ lessons.own_file }}`, and only by appending.** Another session may be
  running beside this one; its file is its record of what it measured and is not yours to
  edit. Delete nothing, including your own entries from an earlier session — a lesson that
  turned out to be wrong is corrected by a later entry saying so and saying why, and the
  pair is worth more than either alone.
- **A lesson tells you which experiment to run, not what it will return.** It can say an
  idea was tried and lost, so try something else or beat it deliberately. It cannot say
  this trial is fast. `benchmark` decides that, every trial, as Rule 3 says.
- **Do not copy the ledger into the kernel.** A tile size, a sub-group width or a
  work-group shape that won on another kernel is a hypothesis here, not a setting.
{% endif %}
## WORKFLOW — Follow these steps in order

### Step 1: Analyze
{%- if not baseline_oracle %}
- Read `test_kernels/{{ kernel_name }}_pytorch.py` — the semantic reference. Identify the
  operations, shapes, dtypes and interface there.
{%- endif %}
- Read `test_kernels/{{ kernel_name }}{{ ext }}` if it exists — that is the baseline to
  optimize.
{%- if kernel_repo %} If it does not exist, write it before Step 2 as a self-contained copy
  of the kernel the `kernel-locator` profile names, in its own language, wrapped as a
  `Model` and built with the flags the profile lists -- the baseline is that kernel, not a
  new implementation. Copy every kernel the op launches, in the order it launches them --
  each one before and after the main kernel as well as the main kernel -- from the
  repository's source; write none of them yourself. Change only what building outside the
  repository requires (includes, a namespace, dispatch branches the workload never takes).
  Every kernel's device code -- the kernel class and the mainloop, epilogue, scheduler and
  reduction it instantiates -- is copied into the file; from the repository only headers of
  the libraries it builds on (CUTLASS/CuTe, SYCL, oneDNN, torch) may be included by `-I`,
  never the op's own kernel headers.
{%- if baseline_oracle %} The file also defines `get_init_inputs()` and `get_inputs()`,
  taking exactly the spec variant's dims as keyword arguments, and builds every input the
  way the repository's own test or call site does -- cite its file:line for each
  structured input (index or block tables, offsets, lengths, scales, routing); random
  values only where the repository uses random values. `Model` keeps its state in
  registered buffers, set from `get_init_inputs()`.
{%- endif %}
{%- if dsl == "sycl" %} `load_inline` gives `extra_sycl_cflags` to the device compile only:
  include directories go in `extra_include_paths` and defines in `extra_cflags` as well,
  or the host pass cannot see them; `-L`/`-l` go in `extra_ldflags`. Device-link flags have
  no parameter: append the SPIR-V extensions the profile lists to
  `torch.utils.cpp_extension._SYCL_DLINK_FLAGS` before `load_inline`, once per target (`-Xspirv-translator=spir64_gen -spirv-ext=...` and
  `-Xspirv-translator=spir64 -spirv-ext=...`), and put ocloc options (e.g. the GRF mode)
  after the device name in `TORCH_XPU_ARCH_LIST`.
{%- endif %}
{%- else %}
{%- if dsl == "sycl" %} If it contains `TODO` markers, this is a from-scratch task: it is
  a working harness (`load_inline`, a `Model` wrapper) with the SYCL kernel and
  `Model.forward` left for you to write. Copy `get_inputs`/`get_init_inputs` and any
  dimension constants in from the reference above rather than importing them — this
  file is compiled and run on its own, with no access to sibling files (Rule 5). Fill
  in both TODOs, confirm it runs correctly, and only then move to Step 2 (`trial
  init`) — this file is the fixed point every trial is measured against, not itself a
  trial.
{%- endif %} If it does not exist at all, this is a from-scratch task with no seed:
  write the initial implementation there yourself, from the reference above, before
  Step 2.
{%- endif %}
- **List `test_kernels/` and read every other file there for this kernel.** The project that
  generated this workspace puts the interface the trial must expose, the benchmark spec and
  any further sources of the baseline there; they are part of the brief, not decoration, and
  nothing repeats them here.
- Run `xe-forge-skill analyze {{ analyze_file }}` for AST-based analysis (it reads PyTorch
  sources; skip it if that file is absent).
{%- if dataset %}
- **Dispatch the `workload-inspector` agent once**, and read the
  `{{ dataset.profile_path }}` it writes before choosing a first strategy. The shapes in
  the spec say what will be timed; the profile says which of them the model actually runs
  and what the tensors look like inside. A kernel written before reading it is written
  against an average of shapes rather than against the work. If the file already exists
  from an earlier session, read it instead of re-running the agent.
{%- endif %}
{%- if kernel_repo %}
- **Dispatch the `kernel-locator` agent once**, and read the
  `{{ kernel_locator_profile_path }}` it writes before choosing a first strategy. This
  kernel already exists in `{{ kernel_repo }}`; the profile says how it is registered,
  built, tested and benchmarked today, so you build on what is real rather than
  reinventing a harness the project already has. If the file already exists from an
  earlier session, read it instead of re-running the agent.
{%- endif %}
{%- if lessons %}
- **Read every file under `{{ lessons.dir }}`** before choosing a first strategy. An idea
  already tried and measured there is one to skip, or to beat on purpose and say so —
  either is cheaper than rediscovering it over three trials. Say in your first trial's
  `--strategy` which ledger entry, if any, it came from.
{%- endif %}
- Read relevant `knowledge_base/` files for optimization patterns. Read each entry's
  `description`, `rationale`, `pattern_before`, `pattern_after`, `applies_to` and any
  prerequisites. **Do not read `expected_speedup`** — it is what a pattern was worth on the
  machine it was recorded on, and whether it is worth anything here is settled by `benchmark`
  on this part and by nothing else.

### Step 2: Initialize
{% if baseline_oracle %}
Before initializing trials, check that the baseline builds, runs on the device and
repeats deterministically -- it is the oracle, so nothing else checks it:
{% elif reference_workload %}
Before initializing trials, verify the generated baseline against the immutable reference:
{% endif %}
{%- if reference_workload %}
```bash
xe-forge-skill benchmark test_kernels/{{ kernel_name }}{{ ext }} test_kernels/{{ kernel_name }}{{ ext }} {{ benchmark_options }}
```
Require `Correctness: PASSED`. Repair the baseline until it passes; static `validate`
does not establish numerical correctness. Do not create optimized trials before this gate.
The self-comparison is a baseline check, not an optimization or a speedup result.
{% endif %}
```bash
xe-forge-skill trial init {{ kernel_name }} test_kernels/{{ kernel_name }}{{ ext }}{% if require_profiles %} --require-profiles ComputeBasic EuStallSampling{% endif %}
```

### Step 3: Trial Loop
For each trial:
1. **Write kernel** — start from the baseline or modify the previous trial.
2. **Validate** — `xe-forge-skill validate <path> --dsl {{ dsl }}` (fix until passing).
3. **Save** — `xe-forge-skill trial save {{ kernel_name }} <path> --parent <id> --strategy "description"`.
4. **Benchmark** (MANDATORY every trial):
  - **Trial t0:** `xe-forge-skill benchmark test_kernels/{{ kernel_name }}{{ ext }} <trial_path> {{ benchmark_options }}`
   - **Trials t1+:** get the cached baseline via `xe-forge-skill trial baseline-us {{ kernel_name }}`, then the same command with `--baseline-us <cached>`
5. **Record** — `xe-forge-skill trial result {{ kernel_name }} <trial_id> --correctness <pass|fail> --speedup <float> --baseline-us <float> --kernel-us <float>`
{%- if measurement == "host" %}
   When the benchmark gated the comparison it reports no speedup. Omit `--speedup`
   entirely and pass `--verdict <the gate it named>` instead. Omitting it is not a
   half-recorded trial: a trial with both times and no ratio ranks **at parity**, above
   every measured regression, so nothing is lost by leaving it out and a number written
   there would be one you did not measure.
{%- endif %}
6. **Profile** — if `unitrace_enabled: true` or `vtune_enabled: true` in `config.yaml`,
  run `xe-forge-skill profile <kernel_file> {{ profile_options }}{% if require_profiles %} --trial-id <trial_id>{% endif %}`
  and use the output to guide the next trial.{% if has_spec %} Use the same `--variant` as the benchmark.{% endif %}
{%- if require_profiles %}
  Pass the saved trial file. Every correct trial that differs from the baseline, including
  the last before finalize, needs both collections attempted; `trial save` and `finalize` refuse otherwise. A failed
  collection is recorded and final: report it as failed, do not retry it, and do not
  drop to a single `--metric-group`.
  Each next trial's `--strategy` cites the profiled measurement it targets (kernel, metric,
  value, trial). After profiling, state the hottest kernel's stall-reason shares beside its
  parent's. If the dominant stall reason's share has not moved over the last two profiled
  trials, the next trial targets that stall reason directly, or the summary says why it cannot.
{%- endif %}
  If both are `false`, skip this step. Collection isolates forward calls from setup and warmup.
{% if reference_workload %}
  The profiler validates the trial against the immutable reference and restores buffers
  outside collection. Forward-only profile timings must not replace the benchmark's
  reset-plus-forward timings or be used to compute trial speedups.
{% endif %}
7. **Decide next action** — from the benchmark output:
   - Correctness FAILED → fix on the same branch. Read the `Output:` block; it carries the
     compiler diagnostic or the mismatch that says what to fix.
   - Speedup improved → continue on this branch.
   - Speedup regressed → branch back to the best trial and try a different strategy.
   - Plateau (several trials on a branch with no improvement) → try a fundamentally
     different approach, not another variation of the same one.
{%- if dataset %} Re-read `{{ dataset.profile_path }}` first: a plateau usually means the
     kernel is tuned for a shape or a data property the workloads do not have.
{%- endif %}
{% if measurement == "host" %}
   - `VERDICT: BELOW_FLOOR` or `VERDICT: INDISTINGUISHABLE`, and `speedup=none` → **this is
     not a regression and not an improvement**. The two arms were closer together than the
     part's measured timing floor, so there is no result. Record it with `--verdict` and no
     `--speedup`, and do not branch away from the kernel on it. Either re-benchmark on a larger
     shape with `--variant <name>` (the spec lists them{% if dataset %}, and
     `{{ dataset.profile_path }}` says which of them carry the real work — pick the largest
     one that is representative, not merely the largest{% endif %}) or stop and say the
     shape is unmeasurable here.
   - `VERDICT: NO_CALIBRATION` or `VERDICT: EXTERNAL_ERROR` → STOP and report. The gate could
     not be evaluated, so no trial after this one means anything.
{% endif %}

**Stop when one of these fires, and name it in the finalize summary:**
- `max_trials` from `config.yaml` is exhausted;
- you have exhausted the ideas this kernel's regime admits — say which you tried and why
  nothing remains;
- the search has plateaued — several consecutive trials, on more than one branch, with no
  measured improvement.

Claim bandwidth saturation only against an independently measured bandwidth reference on
this part; bytes divided by a bandwidth taken from the same run reproduces the elapsed time
by construction and proves no ceiling. Claim a noise band only from repeated `benchmark`
runs of the same arms.

### Step 4: Finalize
```bash
xe-forge-skill trial finalize {{ kernel_name }} output/{{ kernel_name }}_optimized{{ ext }}
```
{% if measurement == "host" %}
If the best trial is a directory, drop the extension — `output/{{ kernel_name }}_optimized` —
and it is written out as a directory holding all of that trial's sources.
{% endif %}
`finalize` refuses to write out a trial slower than the baseline, so a failure here means
either nothing was correct or nothing reached parity — `trial status` says which. That is a
result to report, not a thing to work around.

{% if kernel_repo %}
If finalize wrote `output/`, dispatch the **port-back** agent once. It turns the winner into
`output/upstream.patch` against `{{ kernel_repo }}` without touching the repository. Name
the patch, or the reason there is none, in the summary.
{% endif %}
Then write a short summary: the best trial, its measured speedup — or the gate that withheld
one, said as parity rather than as a win — which stopping condition fired, and what you tried
that did not work.
{% if lessons %}
### Step 5: Record what this run measured

Append one entry to `{{ lessons.own_file }}`, at the end of the file, following the shape
the file's own header gives. It carries:

- the date, and the context a reader needs to know when it applies again: kernel, DSL,
  device, the timer `benchmark` named, and the baseline time and variant it was against;
- **every strategy you tried and what `benchmark` returned for it** — the speedup, or the
  gate that withheld one. The losses matter as much as the win: they are what stops the
  next run spending trials on them;
- anything you learned about **this part rather than this kernel** — a variant too small to
  resolve a difference on, a build that failed for a reason that was not the kernel's, a
  limit the part enforced. This is the part of the entry another kernel's session will read;
- what you did not get to, and why.

Write no number you did not measure and no prediction. An entry saying a strategy "should"
help records a guess in a file whose whole value is that it holds only measurements, and
the next session cannot tell the two apart. If this run measured nothing — everything
gated, or nothing built — write that: it is a fact about this kernel on this part, and it
is the most expensive one to rediscover.

This step runs even when the run went badly. A run that stops early and records why is
worth more to the next one than a run that finished and said nothing.
{% endif %}

## CRITICAL CORRECTNESS CONSTRAINTS
{% if dsl in ("triton", "gluon") %}
- NO default values for `@triton.autotune` meta-parameters in kernel signature
- Use 1D grid when applying tile swizzling (GROUP_SIZE_M)
- `boundary_check` uses dimension indices (0, 1), not booleans
- Cast batch indices to `int64` before stride multiplication
- The kernel module must expose the same entry point as the baseline file it replaces
{% elif dsl == "sycl" %}
- **Give every implementation distinct SYCL kernel identities.** Baseline and trial
  extensions coexist in one process. Use an implementation-specific namespace or prefix
  for every named kernel type passed to `parallel_for`, including unchanged helper kernels.
  Change that identity when the implementation changes. A distinct `load_inline` module
  name alone does not isolate device kernel names; collisions can corrupt either arm.
- **Use the caller's queue.** Do not create a `sycl::queue`. Obtain the one the framework
  already runs on — for a TVM-FFI entry point that is
  `static_cast<sycl::queue*>(TVMFFIEnvGetStream(dev.device_type, dev.device_id))`. A kernel
  that submits to its own queue runs correctly, out of order with everything around it, and
  its measured time is not the time it would take in the model.
- **Do not synchronize inside the kernel function.** No `queue.wait()`, no host-side barrier
  per call: the harness owns synchronization, and a wait inside the call is measured as part
  of the kernel.
- **Sub-group size is declared, not assumed.** Annotate with
  `[[sycl::reqd_sub_group_size(N)]]` and use a size this part actually supports; a
  sub-group reduction written for one width is silently wrong on another.
- **Vectorized access has preconditions.** A `sycl::vec` load or a `sub_group` block read
  requires the pointer to be aligned to the access width and the extent to be a whole
  multiple of it. Guard the tail rather than assuming the shape divides evenly — a shape
  that divides on the benchmarked variant may not on another.
- **Accumulate in a wider type.** Reductions over `half`/`bfloat16` accumulate in `float`;
  accumulating in the storage type passes on small shapes and drifts out of tolerance on
  large ones.
- **Local memory is a resource, not free storage.** Every `local_accessor` byte reduces
  occupancy; a work-group size that fits at one tile size may not at the next.
- **Register-file mode is a per-trial choice, recorded in the trial source** (e.g. the
  `sycl::ext::intel::experimental::grf_size` kernel property). When the profile shows a
  dominant stall reason or an occupancy change, at least one trial varies it or the
  optimization level against its parent, and `benchmark` decides.
- **Tile parameters are measured, not chosen.** A trial built on a work-group size,
  sub-group width, split count or elements-per-step tries at least two values as sibling
  trials from the same parent; a difference inside the measured noise band is no verdict.
{%- if compiler_flags %}
- **Pass `compiler_flags` from `config.yaml` on every compile.** They are additional to
  whatever `-fsycl` invocation you build, not a replacement for it — a floor, not a
  ceiling: add further flags of your own in a trial when that trial's strategy calls
  for one (e.g. a GRF mode or an extra include path), you just must not drop these.
{%- endif %}
{%- if measurement == "host" %}
- **Split the kernel when it helps, and pass the directory.** A header of tile parameters
  or a second translation unit is fine; every source you name is compiled. Keep the
  exported symbol in the entry-point file.
{%- else %}
- **The kernel must be self-contained.** All helpers inline in the one file, because that
  file is what is compiled.
{%- endif %}
{% elif dsl == "cuda" %}
- Use the caller's stream; do not create one, and do not synchronize inside the kernel call.
- Declare launch bounds rather than relying on the compiler's occupancy guess.
- Accumulate reductions over `half`/`bfloat16` in `float`.
- Guard tails explicitly; a vectorized load requires the alignment and extent to permit it.
{%- if compiler_flags %}
- Pass `compiler_flags` from `config.yaml` on every compile; they are additional to whatever
  invocation you build, not a replacement for it.
{%- endif %}
{%- if measurement == "host" %}
- Split the kernel across files when it helps, and pass the directory; keep the exported
  symbol in the entry-point file.
{%- else %}
- The kernel must be self-contained — all helpers inline in the one file.
{%- endif %}
{% endif %}
